      "19255": {
        "comments": [
          {
            "creator": "kevinushey",
            "is_markdown": false,
            "attachment_id": {},
            "time": "2026-10-26T20:03:49Z",
            "creation_time": "2026-10-26T20:03:49Z",
            "count": 0,
            "text": "A syntax error following a `#line` filename directive can leave R's parser error path using a source-file object after it has become unprotected. In a strict-barrier build this produces an \"unprotected object (was STRSXP)\" error instead of the syntax error; without that check, the stale pointer may be read while formatting the error.\n\nReproducible example:\n\n    gctorture2(1L)\n    str2expression(\"#line 1 \\\"srcfile.R\\\"\\n(\")\n\nI reproduced this on an unpatched R-devel r90605 build with --enable-strict-barrier, which reports \"unprotected object ... encountered (was STRSXP)\". The relevant parser code is unchanged at r90764. The expected result is the syntax error beginning \"srcfile.R:2:0: unexpected end of input\".\n\nAnalysis:\n\n`setParseFilename()` in src/main/gram.y puts a replacement filename STRSXP in the parser's source-file state. On a syntax error, `yyerror()` copies that pointer to `R_ParseErrorFile`. `R_Parse()` finalizes its source-reference state before `parseError()` in src/main/source.c formats the message, removing the object's GC root. Allocations in `tabExpand()` can collect it before `getParseFilename()` reads the pointer.\n\nProposed patch: attached as parse-error-srcfile-gc.diff. It preserves the error-file object until its filename has been copied, releases it on error handling and before a later parse, and adds a GC-torture regression test. The patch is against SVN revision 90764.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/412, where CI results can be seen.",
            "bug_id": 19255,
            "tags": [],
            "is_private": false,
            "id": 103966
          },
          {
            "tags": [],
            "attachment_id": 3957,
            "id": 103967,
            "time": "2026-10-26T20:04:01Z",
            "is_private": false,
            "creation_time": "2026-10-26T20:04:01Z",
            "creator": "kevinushey",
            "bug_id": 19255,
            "text": "Created attachment 3957\nprotect parse error filename through parser cleanup",
            "count": 1,
            "is_markdown": false
          },
          {
            "count": 2,
            "is_markdown": false,
            "bug_id": 19255,
            "text": "Created attachment 3958\nkeep the parse error srcfile alive as a GC root\n\nI have replaced the patch with a simpler one. R_ParseErrorFile is a global that holds a SEXP across the gap between yyerror() and parseError(), so rather than juggling R_PreserveObject/R_ReleaseObject around it, the new patch marks it from the collector's root list in memory.c next to R_Srcref, and clears it in parseError() once the filename has been copied so a caught error does not pin the srcfile until the next one. yyerror() and the filename extraction are untouched.\n\nThe regression test in reg-tests-1e.R now also covers the srcfile-environment case (parse(text =, keep.source = TRUE) with a #line directive), which a strict-barrier build reports as an unprotected ENVSXP. Checked on a strict-barrier build at r90764. Attachment 3957 is obsolete.\n\nPatch also at https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/412",
            "creation_time": "2026-10-26T20:43:06Z",
